"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":6,"NextAnswerId":1,"SubSelector":"TX","QuestionText_Unsafe":"<strong>Did the response address what the user asked for?&nbsp;<br><\/strong><em>1 = Not at all, 3 = Partially, 5 = Completely<\/em>","DataExportTag":"P03_V2_A_Helpful","QuestionID":"QID134"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID135","SecondaryAttribute":"How easy is the response to understand? 1\u00a0 = Difficult, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Easy \u00a0","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>How easy is the response to understand?<\/strong><br \/>\n<em>1&nbsp; = Difficult, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Easy<\/em><br \/>\n&nbsp;","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","LabelPosition":"BELOW"},"QuestionDescription":"How easy is the response to understand? 1\u00a0 = Difficult, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Easy \u00a0","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"1"},"2":{"Display":"2"},"3":{"Display":"3"},"4":{"Display":"4"},"5":{"Display":"5"}},"ChoiceOrder":["1","2","3","4","5"],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"ON","ForceResponseType":"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":6,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<strong>How easy is the response to understand?<\/strong><br \/>\n<em>1&nbsp; = Difficult, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Easy<\/em><br \/>\n&nbsp;","DataExportTag":"P03_V2_A_Clarity","QuestionID":"QID135"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID136","SecondaryAttribute":"How warm (friendly or engaging) is the response?\u00a0 1 = Very Cold, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Very Warm\u00a0","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>How warm (friendly or engaging) is the response?&nbsp;<\/strong><br>\n<em>1 = Very Cold, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Very Warm&nbsp;<\/em>","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","LabelPosition":"BELOW"},"QuestionDescription":"How warm (friendly or engaging) is the response?\u00a0 1 = Very Cold, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Very Warm\u00a0","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"1"},"2":{"Display":"2"},"3":{"Display":"3"},"4":{"Display":"4"},"5":{"Display":"5"}},"ChoiceOrder":[1,2,3,4,5],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"ON","ForceResponseType":"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":6,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<strong>How warm (friendly or engaging) is the response?&nbsp;<\/strong><br \/>\n<em>1 = Very Cold, 3 = Nuetral, 5 = Very Warm&nbsp;<\/em>","DataExportTag":"P03_V2_A_Warmth","QuestionID":"QID136"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID137","SecondaryAttribute":"Response A: Great question! When it comes to mosquito bites, it definitely seems like some people...","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<p><strong>Response A:<\/strong><\/p><div style=\"width:100%; max-width:100%; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">Great question! When it comes to mosquito bites, it definitely seems like some people get bitten a lot more than others\u2014and there\u2019s real science behind that.<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">Here are some of the main reasons why mosquitoes are more attracted to certain people:<\/p><ol style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Body Chemistry<\/strong>: Mosquitoes are attracted to chemicals that our bodies naturally give off, such as lactic acid, uric acid, and ammonia, which are found in sweat. Some people naturally produce more of these chemicals, making them more appealing to mosquitoes.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Body Temperature &amp; Heat<\/strong>: People who run hotter or have a higher body temperature can attract more mosquitoes. Physical activity, for example, can make you more of a target because of the heat and sweat you emit.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Carbon Dioxide (CO\u2082)<\/strong>: Mosquitoes can detect the carbon dioxide we exhale from a distance. People who exhale more CO\u2082\u2014like adults and larger individuals\u2014might attract more bites.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Blood Type<\/strong>: There\u2019s some evidence that people with Type O blood get bitten more often than people with Type A or B blood.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Skin Bacteria<\/strong>: The mix and amount of bacteria on your skin can create unique odors that attract mosquitoes. Some skin microbiomes are just more tantalizing to mosquitoes.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Scents (Lotions, Perfume, etc.)<\/strong>: Some scents from personal care products can either attract or repel mosquitoes, so things like fruity lotions or floral perfumes can make you more interesting to them.<\/li><\/ol><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">So, it\u2019s not exactly that some people \u201ctaste\u201d better, but rather that the combination of body chemistry, smells, and heat makes some people more noticeable and inviting to mosquitoes than others.<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">If you\u2019re one of those people who always seems to get bitten, it\u2019s definitely not your imagination\u2014there\u2019s real biology behind it!<\/p><\/div>","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText"},"QuestionDescription":"Response A: Great question! When it comes to mosquito bites, it definitely seems like some people...","ChoiceOrder":[],"Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":4,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<p><strong>Response B:<\/strong><\/p><div style=\"width:100%; max-width:100%; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">Great question! In physics, when figuring out <strong>net force<\/strong> (the overall force acting on an object), the calculations often <strong>use vectors<\/strong>, which are usually represented as <strong>arrows<\/strong> in diagrams or as <strong>lines with direction and magnitude<\/strong>.<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">When it comes to equations and adding forces together, the \"shapes\" they use are often:<\/p><ol style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Straight Line (Linear\/Collinear Forces):<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">If all the forces act in a straight line (like a tug-of-war), you just add or subtract the forces (with direction as positive or negative).<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">Shape used: <strong>straight line<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Parallelogram or Triangle (Non-Collinear Forces):<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul style=\"margin:6px 0 12px 24px; padding:0;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">If the forces act at angles to each other, you often <strong>draw them as arrows from a common point<\/strong> and then use the <strong>parallelogram law<\/strong> or the <strong>triangle law<\/strong> to add vectors.<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\">The most common shapes here are:<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Triangle<\/strong> (triangle law of vector addition)<\/li><li style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>Parallelogram<\/strong> (parallelogram law of vector addition)<\/li><\/ul><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>Summary Table:<\/strong><\/p><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin:8px 0 12px 0;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Situation<\/th><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Shape Used<\/th><th style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; text-align:left;\">Description<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces in one direction<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Straight Line<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces added\/subtracted linearly<\/td><\/tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces at angles<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Parallelogram<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Combine using parallelogram law<\/td><\/tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Forces at angles<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Triangle<\/td><td style=\"border:1px solid #d0d7de; padding:6px 8px; vertical-align:top;\">Combine using triangle law<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>In Equations:<\/strong><br>The net force is often written as:<\/p><pre style=\"margin:8px 0 12px 0; padding:12px; background:#f6f8fa; border:1px solid #d0d7de; border-radius:6px; white-space:pre-wrap; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word;\"><code>\\vec{F}_{\\text{net}} = \\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2 + \\vec{F}_3 + \\cdots<\/code><\/pre><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">This equation implies you are <strong>adding vectors<\/strong>, which are usually represented using the shapes above.<\/p><hr style=\"margin:12px 0; border:none; border-top:1px solid #d0d7de;\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\"><strong>In short:<\/strong><br>They use <strong>lines, triangles, or parallelograms<\/strong> to represent and add forces in net force equations, depending on direction!<\/p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;\">If you want a diagram or example for a specific situation, just ask!<\/p><\/div>","DataExportTag":"P03_V2_ResponseA","QuestionID":"QID137","QuestionJS":false}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID138","SecondaryAttribute":"In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its c...","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its content and the way it is written. There are no right or wrong answers, we are interested in your honest judgment.<\/strong>","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText"},"QuestionDescription":"In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its c...","ChoiceOrder":[],"Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":4,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<strong>In the next few questions, rate Response B based on your overall impression, including both its content and the way it is written. There are no right or wrong answers, we are interested in your honest judgment.<\/strong>","DataExportTag":"P03_Tones_B","QuestionID":"QID138"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID139","SecondaryAttribute":"Did the response address what the user asked for?\u00a01 = Not at all, 3 = Partially, 5 = Completely","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>Did the response address what the user asked for?&nbsp;<br><\/strong><em>1 = Not at all, 3 = Partially, 5 = Completely<\/em>","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","SubSelector":"TX","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","LabelPosition":"BELOW"},"QuestionDescription":"Did the response address what the user asked for?\u00a01 = Not at all, 3 = Partially, 5 = Completely","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"1"},"2":{"Display":"2"},"3":{"Display":"3"},"4":{"Display":"4"},"5":{"Display":"5"}},"ChoiceOrder":[1,2,3,4,5],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"ON","ForceResponseType":"ON","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":6,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionText_Unsafe":"<strong>Did the response address what the user asked for?&nbsp;<br><\/strong><em>1 = Not at all, 3 = Partially, 5 = Completely<\/em>","DataExportTag":"P03_V2_B_Helpful","QuestionID":"QID139"}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID14","SecondaryAttribute":"In the next few questions, please keep in mind your overall impression of Response A & Response B...","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>In the next few questions, please keep in mind your overall impression of Response A &amp; Response B, including both its content and the way it is written. There are no right or wrong answers, we are interested in your honest judgment.<\/strong>","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"Template_Overall","QuestionID":"QID14","QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText"},"QuestionDescription":"In the next few questions, please keep in mind your overall impression of Response A & Response B...","ChoiceOrder":[],"Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":4,"NextAnswerId":1}},{"SurveyID":"SV_elJCHDTDWBcCbD8","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID140","SecondaryAttribute":"How easy is the response to understand? 1\u00a0 = Difficult, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Easy \u00a0","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<strong>How easy is the response to understand?<\/strong><br \/>\n<em>1&nbsp; = Difficult, 3 = Neutral, 5 = Easy<\/em><br \/>\n&nbsp;","DefaultChoices":false,"QuestionType":"MC","Selector":"SAHR","Sub